We study two VLA architectures whose vision connectors differ in how they map encoder patches to language model input tokens (Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}). SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (451.5M parameters) encodes each camera image with SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} into 1024 patches on a $32 \times 32$ grid, then applies a pixel-shuffle connector that folds each $4 \times 4$ block into one token by channel concatenation, producing 64 tokens per camera (128 total from two cameras). Each token encodes 16 spatially contiguous patches; removing one eliminates an entire $4 \times 4$ receptive field with no redundancy. The 128 tokens enter a Qwen2-0.5B backbone~\cite{yang2024qwen2} that generates continuous 7-DoF actions via flow matching~\cite{lipman2023flow}. OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} (7B parameters) uses DINOv2~\cite{oquab2023dinov2} and SigLIP encoders with a three-layer MLP projector (GELU activations) that maps each patch independently into a Llama-2 backbone, producing 256 tokens that preserve per-token independence.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_architecture.pdf}
\caption{Vision connector comparison. SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle folds $4{\times}4$ patches into single tokens (128 total, spatially entangled). OpenVLA's MLP projector maps each patch independently (256 tokens, per-token independence).}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

We evaluate on LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero}, a suite of language-conditioned tabletop manipulation tasks with three suites: Object (10 manipulation tasks), Spatial (10 spatial reasoning tasks), and Goal (10 goal-conditioned tasks). Each condition uses 50 episodes per task (500 per suite), evaluated by closed-loop Success Rate (SR) with Wilson 95\% confidence intervals. SmolVLA baselines: 44.8\% (Object), 51.8\% (Spatial), 63.0\% (Goal); OpenVLA baseline: 74.0\% (Object).

