\subsection{OpenVLA Cross-Architecture Evaluation}
\label{sec:openvla_eval_setup}

We evaluate OpenVLA-7B on LIBERO-Object under random token removal (true removal) at $k \in \{0.30, 0.50, 0.70, 0.90, 0.95\}$, retaining 30\%--95\% of 256 vision tokens. No learned scorer is used; the comparison isolates the vision connector's influence under the simplest pruning strategy.

tool_args.new_string:
\subsection{Cross-Model Pruning Protocol}
\label{sec:openvla_eval_setup}

For cross-model comparison, we apply a unified pruning protocol: compute each vision token's L2 norm, rank tokens, and set the bottom $(1{-}k)$ fraction to zero. OpenVLA is evaluated on LIBERO-Object with random token removal (true removal) at $k \in \{0.30, 0.50, 0.70, 0.90, 0.95\}$. X-VLA uses random token removal at $k \in \{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95\}$; results at $k \leq 0.5$ cover 4 of 10 tasks. $\pi_0$ is evaluated offline only, reporting normalized L1 action loss at $k \in \{0.1, 0.5\}$. OFT provides diagnostic measurements without pruning evaluation.

Two metrics characterize each model's vision token structure from a single forward pass on task-relevant frames. \emph{Attention entropy ratio}: normalized Shannon entropy of attention weights over vision tokens, where low entropy indicates concentrated attention on few tokens. \emph{Token importance CV}: coefficient of variation of L2 norms across vision tokens, where high CV signals uneven importance favorable to pruning.
